 quantum  theory has not  understood it so to try to develop an  intuition for how electrons and other  quantum objects behave let's consider  a simple example this will also help us  to understand what einstein found to be  so objectionable in these new ideas  picture an electron the electron is  described by a wave function  that peaks sharply at two places we'll  call them locations a  and b and let's say that the shape of  the wave function  covers locations a and b equally and to  the same extent  in this case if we were to conduct an  experiment to measure the location of  the electron  there would be a 50 chance that we'd  find it a location a and a 50  chance that we'd find it at location b  at first glance this seems something  like a coin flip  something with two equally likely  outcomes but comparing this to  a coin flip misses something important  about the role that probability plays in  quantum mechanics  so imagine that i flip a coin and then i  cover it up  before seeing which side is up at this  moment i might be tempted to think  that this is like the electron in our  example after all when i uncover the  coin  there'll be a 50 chance it'll be heads  and 50 chance that it will be tails  but there is a crucial difference  between the electron and the coin in  these examples  in the case of the coin the coin is  already in a uniquely heads  or uniquely tails configuration we just  haven't uncovered it yet to find out  which side is facing up  but in the case of the electron it is  not uniquely in location a or uniquely  in  location b it is in both locations a  and b simultaneously according to born  in his way of interpreting quantum  mechanics the electron exists  simultaneously in all of locations that  are covered by the extent  of the wave function  in the case of the coin we're merely  ignorant of which side is facing up  but in the case of the electron it's not  a matter of us not knowing where it's  located  in reality it is not in location a or  location b it is in location a and  location b it is in both locations at  once  in december of 1926 einstein wrote a  letter to max born expressing his  skepticism  of the new interpretation of quantum  theory in this letter  einstein made one of the most famous and  often quoted remarks  let me read you just a few sentences  from this letter  quantum mechanics is very impressive but  an inner voice tells me that it's not  yet the real thing  the theory says a lot but does not bring  us any closer to the secret of the old  one  i at any rate am convinced that he does  not play  dice einstein expressed sentiment  similar to this one on many occasions  throughout his life  in various ways einstein became fond of  insisting that god does not play dice  with the universe  but what exactly did einstein mean when  he said this kind of thing  to understand the nature of einstein's  objection it's important to appreciate  the role that determinism plays  in classical physics according to the  equations of classical physics all  future events at least in principle  can be calculated and predicted with  perfect accuracy  if somehow i could know the exact  location and velocity of every atom and  every other particle in the universe  and if i had access to an infinitely  powerful computer then i could use the  equations of classical physics  to work out everything that would ever  happen in the future  and i could run these equations  backwards to work out everything that  had ever happened in the past  in effect in classical physics the  future is strictly determined by the  present  the universe is like a great and complex  clock simply ticking forward in a  complicated  but entirely predictable manner  this was a vision of the universe that  einstein liked  no no role for chance or probability  according to classical physics  the god of classical physics does not  play dice  but according to max born and his  understanding of quantum mechanics  the universe is not as predictable as it  is in classical physics  think again about our example of an  electron with a wave function that  covers both locations a and b  if we know absolutely everything there  is to know about the electron  there is no way whatsoever to reliably  predict  where we'll find it even if we know the  shape of the electron's wave function  exactly  there's still a 50 chance that we'll  find it at location a  and a 50 chance that we'll find it at  location b  in choosing between these two possible  outcomes the universe is not behaving  deterministically  unlike the deterministic universe that's  described at classical physics  the quantum universe is fundamentally  probabilistic  this is what einstein was talking about  when he objected to god playing dice  with the universe  in his gut einstein felt that the  universe and its laws must be strictly  deterministic  at nature's foundation einstein felt  that there could be no role  for probability or chance  let me take a minute to clarify an  important point about einstein's views  regarding determinism  and probability in some more practical  ways  einstein was perfectly comfortable with  the roles that chance and probability  can play in physics  if i flip a coin there's no practical  way to predict how it will land  in principle this could be reliably  calculated through the laws of classical  physics  but in practice this is an impossible  task there's not a supercomputer in the  world that's powerful enough to  accurately predict  whether a coin will land with its head  or tail side facing up  even an extremely tiny change in how my  thumb strikes the coin  or in the microscopic shape of the coin  or in the distribution of the  surrounding air molecules  could change the outcome of any given  coin flip  so although the laws of classical  physics are strictly deterministic  many situations are in practice highly  chaotic and impossible to predict  this makes it impossible to actually put  this kind of determinism  to real use and einstein knew this and  it this kind of practical indeterminism  didn't bother him at all what did bother  einstein was the prospect of  real indeterminism built deep into the  underlying  laws of physics this kind of  indeterminism would make it impossible  for even an all-knowing being to predict  perfectly something's outcome it was  this kind of indeterminism  that seemed to be present in the new  theory of quantum mechanics  and this brings me to another thing  about this famous einstein  quote and i feel obliged to clarify it  on this point  the statement that god does not play  dice with the universe is often  presented  as proof that einstein was a religious  man or that he believed in god  in fact however this is not at all the  case it's well documented that einstein  didn't subscribe to any traditionally  religious or otherwise theistic beliefs  he did like to use the word god  as an evocative metaphor but he also  made it repeatedly clear that he was not  talking about the god of the bible  or of any other religion when i was  preparing this lecture i had originally  decided that i wouldn't bring up this  point at all  but then i came across a series of  statements by einstein and in these  statements  einstein seemed to be really bothered by  how his remarks were often  misinterpreted in this way  for example there was a 1954 news report  that inaccurately described einstein as  religious  in response einstein wrote the following  remarks  it was of course a lie what you read  about my religious convictions  a lie which is being systematically  repeated  i do not believe in a personal god and  i've never denied this but have  expressed it clearly  if something is in me which can be  called religious then it is  the unbounded admiration for the  structure of the world so far as our  science can reveal it  on this and on many other occasions  einstein made it very clear that he did  not believe in miracles  he did not believe in the existence of  an immortal soul or of any form of  afterlife  other than during his childhood einstein  didn't attend any synagogue or church  or otherwise participate in any  organized religion  he did however frequently refer to the  sense of wonder and awe  that he experienced in contemplating our  universe and its laws  to einstein his science and his religion  were inseparable  okay so back to the debate about the  meaning and interpretation  of quantum mechanics in the late 1920s a  consensus was starting to form  around a refined version of max borne's  interpretation  this view became known as the copenhagen  interpretation named for the city where  niels bohr werner heisenberg  and others had made major contributions  to its development  according to the copenhagen view of  quantum mechanics quantum particles  behave like waves  these particle waves are each described  by their wave function the shape of a  given particle's wave function  represents the probability  that it will be found at different  locations or with different velocities  generally speaking quantum particles  exist in multiple locations at once  and have multiple velocities all  simultaneously  but according to the copenhagen  interpretation whenever an observation  of a particle is made  its wave function collapses and the  measured quantity takes on  a single measured value  in our earlier example prior to any  measurement or observation  the electron is in locations a and b  both simultaneously  but an active observation causes its  wave function to collapse  and its location takes on a single value  either a or b  so how did the scientific consensus come  to form around the copenhagen  interpretation  and what did the physicists working on  quantum mechanics think about the nature  of their theory during this period of  time  the answer to this question probably  depends a lot on when exactly you ask  them  during the late 1920s the theory was  evolving so quickly  that someone could easily have a very  different opinion in 1927  than they did in 1926. furthermore these  scientists were scattered all over  europe  and they generally communicated with  each other through letters and through  publications and scientific journals  these ways of communicating were usually  pretty slow  and they could often leave important  questions unanswered in a way that a  phone call  or better yet an in-person meeting might  not  for all of these reasons scientific  conferences were especially important  in the role in the development of  quantum mechanics and none was more  important or influential  than the fifth solve a conference on  physics which was held in brussels  in october of 1927.  in attendance at this meeting were  nearly all of the major players who'd  been contributing to quantum mechanics  during this period  it was the all-star team of quantum  physics this included einstein of course  but also irwin schroedinger max born  niels bohr louis de broglie  paul dirac and werner heisenberg and  this is not to mention wolfgang paulie  marie curie  and max plonk of the 29 physicists in  attendance  17 of them had either already been  awarded a nobel prize  or would eventually be awarded one  at the 1927 soul they conference it  became clear that a consensus view was  starting to form  around the copenhagen interpretation  most of those in attendance seemed to  have accepted the probabilistic nature  of their new theory there was a general  acceptance  that this was likely to be a real aspect  of nature  and not something that would be  explained away when they better  understood the problem  for example max born werner heisenberg  and niels bohr were each  fully aware that the universe is  described by quantum mechanics was  fundamentally  probabilistic and while they  acknowledged that there was still some  chance  that determinism could somehow be  restored in some future  revision of the theory they each thought  that this was pretty unlikely  unlike einstein they were prepared to  accept the lack of determinism in the  subatomic world  einstein was unwilling to accept any  version of quantum mechanics  in which god played dice with the  universe and when einstein declared this  niels bohr simply replied by telling  einstein  to stop telling god what to do  by this time many physicists including  bohr heisenberg and bourne  felt that the quantum revolution was  coming to a close  they had a perfectly good working theory  and it was not at all clear that any  major new elements were needed beyond  those that they already had  but einstein was still far from  accepting this new consensus view of  quantum mechanics  he felt sure that big pieces of the  theory were somehow still missing  to be clear einstein was not exactly  arguing that the new theory of quantum  mechanics was incorrect  the schrodinger equation the other  equations of this theory described a  great deal of phenomena very well  and the successes of quantum mechanics  were entirely undeniable  so instead of claiming that quantum  mechanics was incorrect  einstein was arguing that it was somehow  incomplete  think back to our example of the  electron that's described by a wave  function  which extends across locations a and b  according to the consensus copenhagen  interpretation  the electron exists in both of these  locations simultaneously  but einstein was skeptical of this maybe  he thought the electron is really in  only one of these places at a given time  but destroying our equation perhaps  couldn't tell us which  if that were true then the apparent  determine in determinism in quantum  mechanics  might just be an illusion he imagined  that there could be another more  complete equation  that would make it possible to calculate  the location of the electron  at a given time without any  probabilistic results  in essence einstein was looking for a  way to make sense of quantum mechanics  that is not only deterministic but in  which the properties of each particle or  object  are always well defined  in early 1927 einstein started working  toward developing a version of quantum  mechanics  that he hoped could explain all of the  observed phenomena of quantum mechanics  while still  allowing the laws of nature to be  strictly deterministic  the class of theories he would end up  developing and later advocating for  became known as hidden variable theories  according to these theories the wave  function of a particle as used in the  schrodinger equation  doesn't tell you everything about that  particle  the wave function was in effect an  incomplete  or partial description of the particle  by hypothesizing  other variables that are missing from  the wave function einstein hoped to  avoid the need for any indeterminism in  the theory  at the solevei conference einstein  argued vigorously that the copenhagen  version of the quantum theory was  fatally flawed  and that a more complete theory was  needed but despite these arguments  he wasn't able to convince many of his  colleagues  in a series of informal but public  discussions with niels bohr  einstein raised what he saw as a series  of major problems with the copenhagen  interpretation  but one after the other bohr responded  effectively to einstein's criticisms  in each case boer found holes in  einstein's arguments  and successfully defended the new  consensus view  by the end of the meeting it was clear  to most of the scientists in attendance  that bohr had bested einstein in these  debates  the copenhagen interpretation is weird  there's no doubt about that  but it also seems to be self-consistent  einstein however was far from giving up  and he was undeterred by his failure at  the solevei conference to demonstrate  any fatal flaws in the copenhagen  interpretation  in the years it followed he continued to  search for a more complete version of  quantum mechanics  that he hoped would restore determinism  to the subatomic world  and furthermore he hoped that such a  theory would allow for a more  straightforward description of electrons  and other quantum particles  he wanted to avoid things like objects  being in multiple places at once  and restored a quantum mechanics what  philosophers sometimes call  realism in the next lecture we'll pick  up where we left off here  we'll talk about einstein's effort and  ultimate failure to build a more  complete  and fully deterministic version of  quantum mechanics  and we'll explore two famous thought  experiments schrodinger's cat  and what's known as the einstein  podolsky rosen paradox  [Music]  you
 

